[SAMPLE] Budget justification
[SAMPLE] Company and PI eligibility evidence', '[SAMPLE] No portal. Do not submit.', '[SAMPLE] U.S. small-business and ownership eligibility must be checked for the actual program.', '[SAMPLE] For STTR, verify institution eligibility, work allocation and IP agreement.', '[SAMPLE] All dates are relative demonstration dates, not official deadlines.', 'irrelevant', '[SAMPLE] Different customer and sensor use case; would add a research-partner dependency.', '[SAMPLE] Fictional training scenario. All company facts, interviews, measurements, prices, schedules and funding examples are invented. No actual application, license, award or customer commitment exists. This exercise is not source material from an agency.', true);

HarborTrace Instruments is a fictional pre-revenue venture. Its product concept is an ammonia-monitoring cartridge for industrial wastewater operators.
Team: Maya Chen (instrumentation and customer discovery), Luis Rivera (sensor chemistry). Both are fictional. A field-operations adviser is still needed. No real entity, registrations, patents, revenues or awards exist.
Current stage: practice Phase I proposal preparation, with unresolved IP access and field reliability. Licensing and proposal work can overlap; funding is not assumed.
Try this route: Dashboard > Discover > Compare > My Projects > Licensing > Funding > Proposal Workspace > Commercialization Roadmap > Documents. The guided-demo banner links every step.
Safe practice: edit the business thesis; complete the technical-criteria task; log an interview; edit a proposal section and export it. All sample dates move relative to the day you create the demo. Remove demo data deletes the entire sample workspace, including your edits, but leaves unrelated work alone.
Decision this week: can the team define an independent real-water test and secure evaluation rights? If not, pause product development.', true);

Three fictional candidates were compared on all ten dimensions with low-confidence evidence. The ammonia cartridge fits the proposed founders and a reachable buyer better than the optical module or corrosion patch. It is not investment-ready. Licensing scores remain low because rights are unknown.
Go/no-go gates: documented evaluation rights; real-water performance within the proposed error/drift limits; two credible pilot sites; plausible maintenance savings. Failure of any gate triggers redesign or stopping, not automatic progression to Phase II.', true);

Research proposal exercise: personnel $120,000 + fringe $30,000 + materials $24,000 + analytical testing $18,000 + travel $6,000 + indirect costs $42,000 = $240,000. No fee or TABA included. All figures are estimates, not an allowable-cost determination.
Company cash scenario, separate from the proposed research award: $90,000 founder capital, $10,000 monthly pre-award burn, nine months of runway if burn is constant. Assume zero grant receipts and zero sales until they occur. A funding delay of four months consumes $40,000 and leaves $50,000. Maintain a three-month ($30,000) reserve; six months of spending reaches that threshold. Do not double-count founder cash as grant revenue.', true);

Hypothetical price: $3,000 installation hardware plus $400/month consumables per site. Hardware cost estimate $1,800 gives $1,200 (40%) gross profit before installation support. Consumables cost estimate $160/month gives $240 (60%) monthly gross profit before field support and warranty.
At ten sites, illustrative hardware revenue is $30,000 and annual consumables revenue is $48,000. The $78,000 total is a scenario, not a forecast or bookings. Support labor could erase the margin. At 500 reachable sites, consumables potential is $2.4M/year; 500 is an unsourced assumption that needs a bottom-up site list.
Test maintenance time, cartridge life, renewal rates, buyer approval limits and channel costs before trusting this model.', true);

Proposed test range 1-50 mg/L NH4-N. Bench target: error <=10% or 0.5 mg/L, whichever is larger. Use paired reference measurements, five cartridges and blanks/standards. Independently define sample handling and interference controls.
Then test three representative wastewater matrices for 30 days, with a target <=10% drift after calibration. Track cleaning minutes, downtime, replacement rates and temperature. No claim of successful testing exists. If drift or fouling fails the gate, pause pilots and revise design.
After permission and safety reviews, plan two 90-day operational pilots. Never use this demonstration as a compliance-reporting method.', true);

Five invented interviews: three confirm the problem, one partly agrees, one does not. Two express conditional pilot interest; zero signed letters, paid orders or committed budgets. This is training material only.
The strongest recurring objection is cleaning and calibration workload. A negative interview shows that a functioning incumbent can make switching unattractive. Next: recruit five genuine operators outside the founder network and ask about current spend, maintenance time, false alarms and purchase authority.', true);

Technical risk: fouling and interference. Owner Luis. Mitigation: paired real-water measurements. Stop if cleaning eliminates the labor benefit.
IP risk: no rights or verified ownership. Owner Maya. Mitigation: confirm claims, owner and evaluation scope. Stop protected-IP testing without permission.
Demand risk: interest may not become budget. Owner Maya. Mitigation: buyer interviews and pilot agreements. Do not call interest revenue.
Financial risk: nine-month initial cash runway, no guaranteed award. Owner Maya. Mitigation: reserve threshold and paid-pilot alternatives.
Regulatory risk: overclaiming compliance use. Obtain appropriate advice and validated methods before making such claims.', true);

This authored exercise asks for a six-month feasibility plan, a $240,000 illustrative budget, a team plan and evidence of IP access. It is not an official solicitation, deadline, eligibility determination or award.
Every proposal checklist item remains "Check solicitation". Before a real application, replace this record with an official source and check amendments, submission window/time zone, cost rules, PI employment, company eligibility and required attachments.', true);

Questions for the actual lab: Who owns the claims? Are there co-owners? What evaluation use is permitted? What data and inventor support are available? What fees, confidentiality limits, reporting and diligence requirements apply? What evidence of IP access will a funder require?
The demo includes only a proposed evaluation pathway and an unsent inquiry. It does not imply rights, permission, exclusivity or lab endorsement. General information, not legal advice.', true);
VALUES (pid, ARRAY[tid,uid2,vid], ARRAY[oid,oid2], lid, prid);
RETURN (SELECT to_jsonb(d) FROM demo_workspaces d WHERE user_id=public.app_user_id());
END $$;
REVOKE ALL ON FUNCTION manage_demo(text) FROM PUBLIC;
GRANT EXECUTE ON FUNCTION manage_demo(text) TO authenticated;
